# The Builder registry

One file, client-side: `src/builder-registry.ts` (`"use client"`). `@builder.io/
sdk-react` cannot register React Server Components — that's this SDK's
constraint, not a Builder-the-product limitation (`sdk-react-nextjs` supports
RSC registration via `isRSC: true`; don't tell a prospect Builder can't do RSC).

## ⚠️ `register("component", info)` alone does not render anything

Confirmed by reading the SDK's compiled source: `register()` writes to an
in-memory store that's only read to `postMessage` the Visual Editor iframe (so
the component shows up in the insert menu and options panel). `<Content>`'s
own render path never reads that store. Call `register("component", ...)` on
its own and the component is fully editable in the Visual Editor — insert it,
fill in every input — and then **renders as nothing**, with only a console
warning to show why:

```
Could not find a registered component named "X".
If you registered it, is the file that registered it imported by the file
that needs to render it?
```

No error, no crash. Easy to ship without ever noticing, since the editor side
looks completely correct.

**The fix — do this for every component:**

```ts
export const CUSTOM_COMPONENTS: RegisteredComponent[] = [
  { component: Hero, name: "Hero", /* ...inputs, etc. */ },
  { component: Section, name: "Section", /* ... */ },
  // add new components here
];

for (const info of CUSTOM_COMPONENTS) {
  register("component", info);
}
```

```tsx
// RenderBuilderContent.tsx
import { CUSTOM_COMPONENTS } from "@/builder-registry";
// ...
<Content content={content} model={model} apiKey={apiKey} customComponents={CUSTOM_COMPONENTS} />
```

Adding a component to `CUSTOM_COMPONENTS` satisfies both halves at once. Never
call `register("component", ...)` by itself outside that array.

## Unconditional registration

Every `register()` call in this file runs at module scope with no `if` guard
of any kind — never conditioned on `builder.editingModel`, a model name, or
anything else. The old build's #1 documented failure was gating its insert-menu
registration on `editingModel === "homepage"`, which hid 9 components on every
other model. There is no acceptable variant of that pattern here.

## Registration call shapes

- **A component:** `register("component", info)` where `info` matches
  `ComponentInfo & { component: YourComponent }` (the `RegisteredComponent`
  type from `@builder.io/sdk-react`). Key fields: `name`, `inputs`, `image`,
  `noWrap`, `canHaveChildren`, `childRequirements`, `group` (groups components
  into their own insert-menu accordion — use `group: "Fieldnote"` for anything
  custom rather than the default "Custom Components" bucket).
- **Editor settings** (design tokens, style strictness): `register(
  "editor.settings", { styleStrictMode, allowOverridingTokens, designTokens })`
  — already set up; extend `designTokens` here when adding a token (see
  `.builder/rules/tokens.mdc`).
- **Inputs:** `{ name, type, defaultValue, enum?, subFields?, showIf?, model? }`.
  `type: "text"` with an `enum: [...]` array renders as a dropdown — there is
  no separate `"enum"` input type. `showIf` takes an actual function,
  `(options: Map<string, unknown>) => boolean`, not just a string expression —
  the SDK serializes functions for editor transport, so a real arrow function
  is both cleaner and fully supported. `type: "reference"` with `model:
  "<model-name>"` renders a content picker scoped to that model.

## Client boundary

`RenderBuilderContent.tsx` is the one place `<Content>` renders, and the one
place `builder-registry.ts` is imported. Pages stay Server Components that
`fetchOneEntry(...)` and pass the result down as a prop — don't fetch inside
the client boundary, and don't move `<Content>` outside it.
